  "body": "Reported by the maintainer in chat on 2026-11-16, with a screenshot:\n\n> bug, In the image we can see the profile of the body wwe expect to see, and in the simulation what we actually see.. The simulation is not properly reflecting the editedd tool\n\nWhat the screenshot shows:\n\n- The tool editor is open on a 6 mm tool. `Holder profile` is `Custom` and reads \"landed: a drawn profile\". The profile table has rows such as `55.0 / 25.0`, `77.5 / 25.0`, `91.5 / 25.0`.\n- The profile preview (125.0 mm overall) shows the drawn holder: a body, a short taper, a wider upper block and a small cap.\n- The parametric fields show `Holder diameter 44.0 mm`, `Holder length 35.0 mm`, `Shank length 40.0 mm`.\n- The simulation in the viewport shows a different tool body: plain stacked cylinders with a wide flange at the top. It does not have the shape of the drawn profile.\n\nExpected:\n\n- The simulation draws the tool, the shank and the holder from the same shape that the tool editor shows.\n- An edit of the tool changes the simulation body without a restart.\n\nFirst step of the work, before any fix:\n\n- Find which holder shape the collision checks and the verify step read for a tool with a custom profile.\n- If they read the drawn profile, this is a display defect and stays `P3`.\n- If they read the parametric fields while a custom profile is set, a holder collision can pass. That is unsafe output: comment the finding on this issue and raise the priority to `P1`.\n\nRelated: #2054 (Weldon holder profiles feed the collision checks), #2007 and its sub-issues (holders referenced from the library).\n\nDone means:\n\n- A test pins that the simulation body of a tool with a custom holder profile has the radii and lengths of that profile.\n- A test pins that an edit of the profile changes the simulation body.\n- A test pins that the collision checks, the verify step and the simulation read one and the same holder shape.\n",
